// Symbol-survival settings for the Runner target.
// Included from Debug.xcconfig and Release.xcconfig, the base configs of the
// Runner target only (RunnerTests has none).
//
// This file used to link libvosk.a (the Kaldi lattice STT engine) with
// -force_load, and was named Vosk.xcconfig. It no longer does:
// asr_catalogue.dart lists the universal sherpa-onnx model (Whisper, 99
// languages) FIRST, and specForLang returns the first match, so the lattice
// engine is unreachable and no vosk_* symbol is ever looked up. Linking a 33 MB
// Kaldi archive for dead code was costing us the whole iOS build — the archive
// referenced 13 kaldi::chain/nnet2 training symbols nothing defined, so the app
// could not link at all. See the restore note at the bottom.
//
// What survives is STRIP_STYLE, which is NOT vosk-specific and must stay:
//
// `flutter build ios` leaves DEPLOYMENT_POSTPROCESSING=NO, so nothing strips
// and the symbols are there. The *archive* action turns it on and runs strip
// with the Xcode default STRIP_STYLE=all, which removes every global symbol and
// rebuilds the export trie without them (measured on the old vosk binary: 14755
// exports -> 671, __text byte-identical — the code survives, the names do not).
// Anything resolved by name at runtime then fails in the App Store build while
// the dev build worked. ja_openjtalk.xcframework resolves its ja_frontend_*
// entry points through DynamicLibrary.process() (see
// native/sherpa_onnx_ios_patched/SETUP.md), which is exactly that pattern.
//
// `non-global` is `strip -x`: drops local symbols, keeps the globals dlsym reads.
STRIP_STYLE = non-global

// ── restoring the lattice (vosk) engine, if ever needed ──────────────────────
// asr_catalogue.dart says the per-language models come back "in one line" by
// moving the universal spec to the END of _specs. On iOS that is now TWO steps,
// because the engine is no longer linked:
//   1. bash scripts/build_libvosk_ios.sh   (rebuilds ios/Frameworks/libvosk.a)
//   2. re-add to Debug.xcconfig + Release.xcconfig:
//        OTHER_LDFLAGS = $(inherited) $(VOSK_LDFLAGS)
//      with, back in this file:
//        VOSK_KEEP    = -u _vosk_model_new -u _vosk_model_free
//                       -u _vosk_recognizer_new
//                       -u _vosk_recognizer_accept_waveform_f
//                       -u _vosk_recognizer_result
//                       -u _vosk_recognizer_partial_result
//                       -u _vosk_recognizer_final_result
//                       -u _vosk_recognizer_reset -u _vosk_recognizer_free
//                       -u _vosk_set_log_level
//        VOSK_LDFLAGS = -force_load $(SRCROOT)/Frameworks/libvosk.a
//                       $(VOSK_KEEP) -lc++ -framework Accelerate
//      (one line each — wrapped here only for readability)
// Both flags were needed, not one, and getting only the first is a green build
// that crashes with "Failed to lookup symbol" on the first call: -force_load
// pulls every archive member in (OpenFST registers its FST types from global
// constructors nothing references, so without it model loading fails at
// runtime), and -u marks each entry point as a dead-strip ROOT, because
// DEAD_CODE_STRIPPING=YES runs *after* -force_load and would otherwise drop all
// ten vosk_* symbols as unreachable from _main. -lc++ because Kaldi/OpenFST are
// C++ and Runner is otherwise ObjC/Swift; Accelerate for Kaldi's BLAS/LAPACK.
// Keep the -u list in sync with the lookupFunction calls in
// lib/swayco/asr/lattice_asr_engine.dart, and verify against the APP binary,
// not the archive:
//     nm -gU build/ios/iphoneos/Runner.app/Runner | grep vosk_model_new
